Numerical Integration on Bounded Domains via Learned Reflected Diffusions Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.40499v1 [cs.LG] 29 Oct 2026 \workshoptitle AI for Stochastic Dynamics Numerical Integration on Bounded Domains via Learned Reflected Diffusions Kirill Korolev Affiliation: HSE University Email: kkorolev@hse.ru Artur Goldman Affiliation: ETH Zürich, ETH AI Center Email: artur.goldman@ai.ethz.ch Timofei Gritsaev Affiliation: Constructor University Email: tgritsaev@gmail.com Tigran Ramazyan Affiliation: HSE University Email: tramazyan@hse.ru Nikita Morozov Affiliation: HSE University Email: nvmorozov@hse.ru Abstract We study high-dimensional integration on bounded convex domains. Given a non-negative integrand r r , the goal is to estimate Z = ∫ 𝒟 r ⁡ ( x ) ​ 𝑑 x Z=\int_{\mathcal{D}}r(x)\,\mathrm{d}x on 𝒟 ⊂ ℝ d \mathcal{D}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d} . Standard Quasi-Monte Carlo does not adapt its evaluation points to the integrand, while effective importance sampling requires a proposal that captures its concentration. Learned diffusion samplers can supply such a proposal, but existing methods are formulated on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} and do not respect the domain constraint x ∈ 𝒟 x\in\mathcal{D} . We instead build the proposal from reflected dynamics that stay in 𝒟 \mathcal{D} by construction. A reflected diffusion progressively transforms the target distribution toward the uniform distribution on 𝒟 \mathcal{D} , and a neural score model is trained to reverse this process. A change-of-measure formula for reflected diffusions yields a training objective without additional boundary terms and an unbiased importance-sampling estimator of Z Z . For non-smooth integrands, where reparameterization gradients may be undefined or uninformative, we derive a REINFORCE estimator of the training gradient using Gaussian proposal densities before reflection, without evaluating reflected transition densities or differentiating r r . We evaluate on numerous synthetic targets and real-world problems, demonstrating strong performance of the proposed method, as well as favorable scaling with the dimension of the problem and the number of points used for the Monte Carlo estimate. 1 Introduction We consider the problem of estimating high-dimensional integrals over bounded constrained domains. Let 𝒟 ⊂ ℝ d \mathcal{D}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d} be a bounded convex domain, and let r : 𝒟 → ℝ ≥ 0 r:\mathcal{D}	o\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} be measurable and satisfy ∫ 𝒟 r ⁡ ( x ) ​ 𝑑 x < ∞ \int_{\mathcal{D}}r(x)\,\mathrm{d}x<\infty . Our goal is to estimate the integral Z = ∫ 𝒟 r ⁡ ( x ) ​ 𝑑 x . Z=\int_{\mathcal{D}}r(x)\,\mathrm{d}x. (1) Integrals of this form are a computational bottleneck across the sciences. In statistical physics and chemistry, free energies and thermodynamic observables are logarithms of such constants, and learned samplers have been used to estimate them for many-body systems and lattice field theories ( Noé et al., 2019 ; Wirnsberger et al., 2020 ; Nicoli et al., 2021 ) . In computer graphics, every pixel of a rendered image is an integral over light paths, parameterized so that the integration domain is the unit cube ( Müller et al., 2019 ) . In computational finance, the price of a path-dependent derivative is an expectation over a discretized asset path, which the standard inverse-CDF construction turns into an integral over [ 0 , 1 ] d [0,1]^{d} with d d the number of monitoring dates ( Glasserman, 2004 ; Dick et al., 2013 ) . In Bayesian statistics, the model evidence is an integral of an unnormalized posterior ( Kloek & Van Dijk, 1978 ; Kass & Raftery, 1995 ) , confined to a bounded region whenever the parameters are subject to simplex constraints or under compactly supported priors. These tasks give rise to integration problems on bounded domains, where the location of integrand mass is unknown in advance. This motivates samplers that respect the domain constraints and adapt to where the mass lies, including when it concentrates near faces or corners, or when the integrand vanishes on parts of the domain. Existing estimators differ in how much they adapt to r ⁡ ( x ) r(x) . Standard Quasi-Monte Carlo ( Niederreiter, 1992 ; Dick et al., 2013 , QMC) spreads a deterministic point set over the cube without reference to the integrand. Bayesian quadrature ( O’Hagan, 1991 ; Rasmussen & Ghahramani, 2003 ) adapts by placing a Gaussian process prior on r ⁡ ( x ) r(x) , but at a cost cubic in the number of evaluations ( Briol et al., 2019 ) . Importance sampling relies on a proposal distribution whose choice determines the estimator’s variance ( Hammersley & Handscomb, 1964 ) . Neural proposals learn that proposal from evaluations of r ⁡ ( x ) r(x) alone, either with normalizing flows ( Rezende & Mohamed, 2015 ; Müller et al., 2019 ) or with diffusion samplers ( Zhang & Chen, 2022 ; Vargas et al., 2023 ; Berner et al., 2024 ) . The latter avoids the invertibility constraints that limit flow architectures ( Kong & Chaudhuri, 2020 ) . Diffusion samplers, however, are formulated on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} with a Gaussian reference process throughout the existing work and do not respect the constraint x ∈ 𝒟 x\in\mathcal{D} . A more thorough discussion of prior work on numerical integration methods is presented in Appendix A . To design a learnable diffusion-based proposal that respects the domain constraint, we draw inspiration from diffusion models ( Song et al., 2021 ) for constrained generative modeling. Existing approaches fall into two groups. The first changes the geometry in which the process evolves: mirror diffusion models map 𝒟 \mathcal{D} onto ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} through the gradient of a strictly convex potential and run a standard diffusion in the resulting dual space ( Liu et al., 2023 ) , while log-barrier diffusions equip the interior of 𝒟 \mathcal{D} with the Riemannian metric induced by the Hessian of a barrier function, under which the process remains in the interior ( Fishman et al., 2023 ) . The second group keeps the Euclidean geometry of 𝒟 \mathcal{D} and confines trajectories by reflecting them at the boundary, using reflected Brownian motion as the noising process ( Fishman et al., 2023 ; Lou & Ermon, 2023 ) . In this work, we follow the second route and adapt reflected diffusions from generative modeling to sampling, where no samples from π \pi are available and the reverse dynamics must be learned from evaluations of r r alone. We outline our contributions as follows: • We propose reflected diffusion samplers (RDS) for integration on bounded domains, extending control-based diffusion samplers ( Zhang & Chen, 2022 ; Vargas et al., 2023 ; Berner et al., 2026 ) to constrained 𝒟 \mathcal{D} . Our construction uses reflected Brownian motion with a time-dependent noise schedule. When initialized from the uniform distribution ν 
u on 𝒟 \mathcal{D} , this process preserves uniform marginals ( Loper, 2020 ) and defines a tractable reference path measure for the integral ( 1 ). Girsanov’s theorem ( Le Gall, 2016 ) then transfers this representation to equivalent controlled reflected diffusions through path-space importance weights. We show that the unique zero-variance ideal proposal corresponds to the same reflected noising dynamics initialized from the normalized target π ⁡ ( x ) = r ⁡ ( x ) / Z \pi(x)=r(x)/Z , and propose to learn its reversal with a neural network-parameterized drift. The learned reversal by construction also acts as the sampler from π \pi . • We design a training objective for the learned reversal to be the KL divergence to the ideal proposal. For smooth targets, its gradients can be computed via the reparameterization trick. It includes a running control cost with a terminal potential and no boundary term, closely resembling the optimal control objective of Zhang & Chen (2022) . We also derive a REINFORCE gradient estimator with a leave-one-out baseline, which uses only the pre-reflection Gaussian transition densities and does not require gradients of r ⁡ ( x ) r(x) , applying to targets that are non-smooth or vanish to zero on part of 𝒟 \mathcal{D} , where reparameterization gradients are uninformative. Integrands that vanish on part of the domain are handled by adding a positive offset and correcting the resulting integral estimate. • We provide rigorous theoretical derivation of all components of the proposed methodology, adapting existing theoretical results to the considered setting of the variance exploding reflected diffusion samplers. In addition, we prove a total-variation accuracy bound for the designed estimator, which is a reflected version of the bound presented in Guo et al. (2026) for diffusion samplers in ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} . • We evaluate on Genz integrands ( Genz, 1984 ) , bounded synthetic targets, Feynman parameter integral ( Bonciani et al., 2016 ) and Asian option pricing ( L’Ecuyer, 2009 ) against Quasi-Monte Carlo, Bayesian quadrature and normalizing flow importance sampling baselines. The experiments demonstrate strong performance of the proposed method, as well as favorable scaling with the dimension of the problem and the number of points used for the estimate. In addition, we compare against Path Integral Sampler (PIS, Zhang & Chen (2022) ) proposal by mapping 𝒟 \mathcal{D} onto ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , and demonstrate that the learned RDS proposal achieves lower integration errors against its unconstrained counterpart. Source code: github.com/kkorolev1/reflected-diffusion-samplers . 2 Background on Reflected SDE Denote 𝒟 ¯ \overline{\mathcal{D}} and ∂ 𝒟 \partial\mathcal{D} to be the closure and boundary of 𝒟 \mathcal{D} , and vol ⁡ ( 𝒟 ) \vol(\mathcal{D}) to be its volume. Consider a reflected SDE on 𝒟 \mathcal{D} with drift f : 𝒟 ¯ × [ 0 , 1 ] → ℝ d f:\overline{\mathcal{D}}	imes[0,1]	o\mathbb{R}^{d} and scalar diffusion coefficient g : [ 0 , 1 ] → ℝ g:[0,1]	o\mathbb{R} ( Pilipenko, 2014 ) : d ​ X t = f ⁡ ( X t , t ) ​ d ​ t + g ⁡ ( t ) ​ d ​ W t + d ​ L t . \mathrm{d}X_{t}=f(X_{t},t)\,\mathrm{d}t+g(t)\,\mathrm{d}W_{t}+\mathrm{d}L_{t}. (2) Here W t W_{t} is a standard d d -dimensional Brownian motion. Under the smooth-boundary assumptions used for the theoretical results, a solution of SDE ( 2 ) has the form ( X , ℓ ) (X,\ell) , where ℓ \ell is continuous, nondecreasing, starts from zero, and increases only when X t ∈ ∂ 𝒟 X_{t}\in\partial\mathcal{D} . Throughout the manuscript, the associated vector reflection term is the derived process L t := ∫ 0 t 𝐧 in ​ ( X s ) ​ d ​ ℓ s L_{t}:=\int_{0}^{t}\mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}}(X_{s})\,\mathrm{d}\ell_{s} , where 𝐧 in \mathbf{n}_{\mathrm{in}} is the inward unit normal. Thus d ​ L t \mathrm{d}L_{t} pushes X X inward at boundary times. Let μ t := Law ⁡ ( X t ) \mu_{t}:=\Law(X_{t}) be the marginal law of X t X_{t} . Suppose that, for t > 0 t>0 , μ t \mu_{t} admits a dens