ty across independent evaluation runs. Figure 2 : Mean absolute error as a function of the number of samples used for estimation on representative benchmarks. Results for FFBQ are missing on some plots due to memory limitations of this algorithm in large dimensions. Baselines . We compare the reparameterization and reinforced variants of our method, denoted RDS-RP and RDS-RF, respectively, against Path Integral Sampler ( Zhang & Chen, 2022 , PIS) , Quasi-Monte Carlo ( Niederreiter, 1992 ; Dick et al., 2013 ) , Fast Fourier Bayesian Quadrature ( Warren & Ramos, 2024 , FFBQ) , and the normalizing flow importance sampling library ZüNIS ( Deutschmann & Götz, 2024 ) . For PIS, we map z ∈ ℝ d z\in\mathbb{R}^{d} to x ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] d x\in[0,1]^{d} with the sigmoid transformation x = σ ⁡ ( z ) x=\sigma(z) and train on the change-of-variables target r ~ ​ ( z ) = r ⁡ ( σ ⁡ ( z ) ) ​ | det J σ ​ ( z ) | \widetilde{r}(z)=r(\sigma(z))\left|\det J_{\sigma}(z)ight| , which preserves the original integral. We report the absolute normalizing-constant error Δ ​ Z = | Z ^ − Z | \Delta Z=|\widehat{Z}-Z| , and show mean and standard deviation across independent evaluation runs. Full experimental details are in Appendix F.2 . Overall, RDS performs well across a broad range of targets with substantially different geometry. In particular, it remains competitive on smooth, multimodal, boundary-concentrated, and high-dimensional problems, indicating that the learned proposal is not specialized to a particular class of integrands. The strong performance across both the dimension and target-evaluation sweeps also shows that the method can effectively adapt its sampling distribution to the regions that contribute most to the integral. Figure 1 illustrates t
